t me think: they call it \"1 additional pass\" meaning one extra forward-backward pass over the whole pipeline, i.e. cost (f+b) per stage, during which the wu w is executed (interleaved in the idle gaps). Hmm, but that's a small cost: extra (f+b) per stage per minibatch to hide w. Then iteration time ≈ (m+1)(f+b) (if w ≤ f+b it's fully hidden) → bubble ratio ≈ ... For ZB(2p): 2 additional passes, each with half the work? No — ZB(2p) splits each of fwd/bwd? Let me recall: \"ZB(2p) requires two additional passes, each of which has half the size (i.e., half the microbatches?)... \" Hmm.\n\nActually I recall Fan et al. state: \"we introduce two additional forward-backward passes... In the first additional pass we apply the optimizer step... ZB(2p) splits the additional pass into two halves, each with half the computation, so that the pipeline can be filled with fewer microbatches — achieving lower m* threshold and no additional delay.\" Something like: ZB(2p) uses two additional passes each of cost (f+b)/2 (half-computation passes, e.g. by splitting each layer's forward into two chunks — like splitting the microbatch's layers), so the effective overhead per iteration is the same (f+b) total but the schedule constraints are looser, making it work with fewer microbatches and reducing the effective pipeline latency.\n\nGiven that the researcher explicitly fixed the definition in their request: \"ZB(1p): 1 paso completo adicional de fwd+bwd+update; ZB(2p): update partido en 2 pasos de coste w/2 cada uno, solapados en huecos\". Hmm, they framed ZB(2p) as: update split into 2 half-cost steps overlapped in gaps. I must follow the researcher's fixing (they said these are mandatory design decisions). But I should be careful and faithful: I'll present the adopted definition exactly as the researcher fixed it, and note in the sensitivity axis that an alternative reading of ZB(2p) (two additional half-length fwd+bwd passes) exists and is covered by the third sensitivity axis. That'