n2. **1F1B-I** (Megatron interleaved / virtual pipeline stages, v model chunks per device):\n   - Placement: each device owns v chunks; round-robin over (chunk, microbatch) pairs; warmup depth (p−1)/v forwards; alternating after that.\n   - Validity: **m must be a multiple of p** (Megatron constraint: num_microbatches % num_model_chunks... actually Megatron requires m divisible by p for the interleaved schedule with the standard microbatch group size = p), and m ≥ p.\n   - Update cost: w per minibatch, single, at the end (1 pass).\n   - C ≈ ((m + (p−1)/v)(f+b)) + w → bubble ratio ≈ (p−1)(f+b)/(v·(m+(p−1)/v)(f+b)) ~ (p−1)/(v·m + p − 1). So bubble divided by v.\n\n3. **ZB-1p** = ZB(1p) (Fan et al. 2023):\n   - Placement: 1F1B-like backbone but each device, whenever it would be idle, runs an auxiliary forward/backward pass (extra \"1 pass\") on the *same* microbatch data using current weights, and applies its weight update in the freed slot; fwd/bwd/wu interleaved so that each device has no idle slot.\n   - Validity: m ≥ p; auxiliary pass requires at least p in-flight microbatches to fill; no divisibility requirement beyond that (adopted).\n   - Update cost: **one extra full fwd+bwd+update pass per minibatch** → total work (m+1)(f+b) + (m+1)·w... hmm. Fan et al.: ZB(1p) requires 1 additional forward-backward pass. The extra pass computes gradients that are... Actually in ZB-H1/H2 (Qi et al.) they do the weight update after each microbatch's bwd, which costs w·m but doesn't need an extra pass. In Fan et al. ZB(1p)/ZB(2p), the extra passes are needed because the wu cannot be applied mid-minibatch (stale weights): you add 1 (or 2) extra forward-backward passes so the pipeline can keep flowing while weights are updated. The \"1p\" naming = 1 additional pass. ZB(2p) = 2 additional passes, each of half cost (f/2, b/2) → so total extra compute is the same as one pass, but the granularity is halved, allowing it to fit in fewer-microbatch schedules and halving the per-device me